  <h1>Common Lemon Law Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>&ldquo;The manufacturer has to give me a full refund, no deductions.&rdquo;</h2><p>Most states allow a mileage offset: the refund is reduced by the proportion of the vehicle&rsquo;s useful life already consumed. California uses 120,000 miles as the denominator. Some states have no offset; others use different formulas. Check your state&rsquo;s specific law.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;Used cars aren&rsquo;t covered.&rdquo;</h2><p>State lemon laws typically cover new vehicles, but the federal Magnuson-Moss Warranty Act covers any vehicle sold with a written warranty — including used vehicles. Some states&rsquo; lemon laws also cover certified pre-owned vehicles still under the original manufacturer&rsquo;s warranty.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I have to use the manufacturer&rsquo;s arbitration program.&rdquo;</h2><p>In California, arbitration is optional for the consumer &mdash; you can go directly to court. Other states require you to attempt manufacturer arbitration first (but the result is not binding on the consumer). Never sign anything that waives your right to a court trial.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I&rsquo;ll have to pay an attorney.&rdquo;</h2><p>In virtually all state lemon law cases, if you win, the manufacturer pays your attorney fees. Lemon law attorneys know this and often take cases without upfront payment. Even if your case isn&rsquo;t a clear winner, a free consultation is worth getting.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;The manufacturer will fix it eventually.&rdquo;</h2><p>That may be true, but after the lemon law threshold is met, you have legal rights. Continuing to accept repair attempts beyond the threshold without asserting your rights can complicate the claim. Consult an attorney once you&rsquo;ve documented the required number of repair attempts.</p>
